gpu_reference_2026-09-17.tsv

The JPEG AI reference software decoding the same four streams on the CPU and on the GPU, so the
port's GPU backend has something to be measured against. Produced by
`scripts/bench/reference_gpu.sh 3`.

reference: ~/work/zen/jpeg-ai-reference-software at b9e573f, its .venv with torch 1.10.2+cu113
  (CUDA 11.3 runtime) on the NVIDIA 580.178.04 driver, CUDA 12.4 toolkit installed on the host.
gpu: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080, 8 GiB. cpu: Ryzen 9 9950X3D.
host: dev, Ubuntu, kernel 7.0.0-31-generic, shared with other agents.
commit (of this repo, for the rows it is compared against): 711233ad

How to read `total_s`: it is the reference's own "TOTAL", the wall time of `decode_stream` —
headers, entropy decoding, the networks and the output stage, but not model loading, hashing or
PNG writing. `process_decoder` calls `torch.cuda.synchronize()` before stopping that clock when
the device is the GPU (src/reco/coders/decoder.py), and calls `init_cuda()` before starting it,
so it is a synchronised measurement of GPU work, not a host timer reporting submission.

Three things worth knowing, all verified here:

1. **`-target_device gpu` alone is not enough.** `Coder.setup_device_param`
   (src/codec/coders/coder.py) forces `target_device` back to `cpu` unless `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`
   is set and non-empty, and says nothing when it does. A run without it produces CPU timings and
   CPU MD5s under a `gpu` command line. The script always sets `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0`; a run
   really on the GPU shows the process in `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps` holding ~1.4 GiB.
2. **The GPU path is deterministic** — three runs per stream, identical MD5 triples each time —
   **but it does not reproduce the CPU path's output**: every stream's GPU MD5 differs from its
   CPU MD5. cuDNN and oneDNN do not sum in the same order; the reference has no bit-exactness
   between its own two devices, which is the same class of difference this port records against
   it in PORTING.md.
3. **The reference's CPU path is single-threaded** — `decode_stream` calls
   `torch.set_num_threads(1)` for `target_device == cpu` — so its CPU column is a 1-thread
   number and belongs next to this crate's `cpu_1t_ms`, not `cpu_mt_ms`.

Medians of the three runs, seconds, against gpu_decode_2026-09-17_rtx2080.tsv's `decode` rows
(whole codestream to 8-bit output, same streams, same box):

  stream                  size       ref CPU 1T  ref GPU  ours CPU 1T  ours CPU 8T  ours GPU
  img30_simple (SOP)      560x888         0.133    0.087        0.056        0.018     0.017
  img30_base   (BOP)      560x888         0.221    0.093        0.091        0.025     0.023
  img30_high   (HOP)      560x888         2.358    0.160        1.327        0.387     0.274
  img01_base   (BOP)     2096x1400        1.224    0.288        0.531        0.155     0.150

The reference's GPU path has a floor of about 85 ms per picture (SOP, where there is almost no
work to do) that is Python and torch per-call overhead, not arithmetic; that is most of why the
two light operating points look so different. HOP is where the comparison is about kernels, and
there the reference's cuDNN path is 1.7x faster than this crate's WGSL kernels (0.160 s against
0.274 s) on the same card, while this crate's CPU engine is 6.1x faster than the reference's CPU
engine (0.387 s against 2.358 s, and 1.8x at equal thread count).

Our columns are the `decode` rows of gpu_decode_2026-09-17_rtx2080.tsv, i.e. the same four
streams decoded end to end. They include the same stages the reference's TOTAL does. The GPU
column there runs the CPU entropy stage and the GPU synthesis in one thread of control with no
overlap, so on SOP and BOP, where synthesis is 4 to 11 ms of a 17 to 23 ms decode, the entropy
stage is what the total is made of and the two columns are nearly equal.
